// ../07-trust-decay.dw with `unless` turned to `when` -- the one change.
//
// THE ARTICLE'S STATED INTENT, verbatim:
//   "After 15 minutes without advisor interaction, the agent loses access to write operations."
//
// `unless { B }` blocks a rule when B holds, so the published policy permitted the trade only when
// the advisor had NOT interacted in the last 15 minutes: trust decayed into more access. With
// `when`, the trade is permitted only while the advisor has interacted within the last 15 minutes,
// which is the sentence. Checked against TrustDecay.tla beside it: both of its claims hold.
//
// The resource scope is dropped, as in ../07-trust-decay.dw.
//
// AND THE ADVISOR INTERACTION ITSELF IS PERMITTED, which the published file did not need: its
// `unless` opened the trade when there was NO advisor response. Gated the right way round, the
// trade needs a response, and with nothing permitting `interact_advisor` the call is denied, no
// response ever exists, and the rule can never fire -- Anchor reports it VACUOUS, the same
// composition effect README.md describes for every fragment here.

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"interact_advisor",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"execute_trade",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 15m AgentCore::Action::"interact_advisor"::response{ }
};
